From 9ff387b92d3d164a0a626ad33c8fc9bd559e1b67 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 10:55:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(backups): derive the nightly backup date from the fire
 time
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The nightly pg_dump has been overwriting itself. `worker.ts` registered the
repeatable job with `data: { date: new Date()... }` — evaluated once, at
worker boot — and BullMQ replays that payload on every fire. The R2 key is
`backups/<date>.sql.gz`, so every night wrote to whichever day the worker
last restarted on.

Nothing failed. pg_dump exited 0, the upload completed, the log said
"[backup] complete", and the previous night's restore point was gone. The
production logs show the run OF 14 Aug stamped `backup_date 2026-08-13`,
because the worker had booted on the 13th — the 13th's backup was
overwritten and no 14th ever existed. The number of restore points equalled
the number of worker restarts, not the number of nights.

The date now comes from `job.timestamp`, this iteration's scheduled fire
time, via `resolveBackupDate`. That value is stable across retries, so a
retried attempt rewrites its own key rather than opening a second one, and
it is read in UTC to agree with the `tz: 'UTC'` cron. An explicit date in
the payload still wins, so a manual re-run can target one day — the
behaviour `pgDumpPayloadSchema` documented but never had, since the field
was required and the scheduler always filled it.

BK-E01/E01b/E02/E02b cover the derivation and prove one boot payload now
yields distinct keys on consecutive nights. BK-E03 scrapes the registration
in worker.ts and fails if a date is ever baked back in — the pure-function
tests cannot see that line, and that line is what cost a week of backups.

Found while investigating FREEHS-5 (an unrelated one-off AccessDenied on
8 Aug, since resolved); this was never reported by anything, because a
backup that silently overwrites its predecessor looks exactly like a
backup that works.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013at7aR3SNsBfccWoVh8pp9
